So, we have previously agreed on implementing the analytics to measure how many users fall into this case when:

* rider got an active order
* rider terminated the app
* driver canceled the order
* rider did not get the push about that ({{Unfortunately your driver had to cancel. Please request a new ride!}})
* rider opens the app and sees no order while expecting to have one

To implement the analytics properly, we need to detect that the mobile app receives (or does not receive) the push about the fact that the driver has just canceled the order. The problem is that the push about this does not contain order handle:

{noformat}[AnyHashable("type"): driver_rejected, AnyHashable("aps"): {
    alert =     {
        body = "Unfortunately your driver had to cancel. Please request a new ride! \Ud83d\Ude4f";
        title = "";
    };
    badge = 1;
    category = "driver_rejected";
    sound = "capisci.caf";
}]{noformat}

In other words, we can not 100% map this push to the order that was canceled because we got no {{order_handle}} in the push’s payload.

What the next steps can be? I see three options:

# Reject this task due to low impact.
# Implement analytics with assumption that the {{driver_rejected}} push 100% related to the ride that we just recently got as active.
# Ask rides team to add {{order_handle}} to the push, and then implement the analytics.

I guess, the first option would be the most reasonable. Why? I got no data to proof my point, but a couple of things:

* in my experience, APN is a fairly stable thing and delivers pushes as expected
* the fact that [~accountid:5d6cb90af989e00d8c7e661c]  didn’t see the push in [https://taxify.atlassian.net/browse/TECH-16773|https://taxify.atlassian.net/browse/TECH-16773|smart-link]  was because we had no {{remoteAsLocalNotificationScheduler}} at that time
** the build from the TECH report was CI.4.47
** and the showing of remote notifications as the local ones was added in CI.4.63 [https://taxify.atlassian.net/browse/ORDER-25|https://taxify.atlassian.net/browse/ORDER-25|smart-link] )
* the pushes were actually sent and received by the app, but again because of the previous point, they were not presented (see the attached PDF for the highlighted moments from the log)



So, in my opinion, since we got the mapper from remote to local pushes (when app is active) and there are no reports about this issue, we can reject it.

