Update 4 October - Response from outside counsel (DLA):

The subject matter is complex and therefore we will require some additional time to provide our opinion on the questions raised by you.

Notwithstanding of the above, we confirm that the procedural law foresees an automatic prohibition to implement a “norm” +in relation to a particular case+ (not in general) when an injunction is filed by someone that is willing to challenge the legality of such “norm”. 

There are some important remarks in this regard:

(i)               The concept of “norm” mentioned above refers to “administrative norms” (i.e., norms enacted by the Administration)  and not “legislative norms” (i.e., norms enacted by legislative bodies);

(ii)              Some legislative acts include “norms” that +in substance+ relate to the administrative function and could potentially be seen as “administrative norms” inside legislative acts;

(iii)             In this particular case, article 11 of the Regional Legislative Decree nr. 14/2020/M (legislative act) has been implemented by the Implementing Decree nr. 1/2020/M, of 25 January 2021 (administrative activity); The Implementing Decree replicates article 11 and regulates its implementation; So we believe that the injunction is related to Implementing Decree (and indirectly to the Regional Legislative Decree) (+this is something it would be important to confirm+);

(iv)             If an injunction is filed and not immediately rejected by the Court (as we understand it is the case), there is an automatic prohibition on the Administration to implement the norm; This prohibition takes effect upon the service of process for the Defendant (Administration); The Defendant shall then ensure that its services refrain from implementing the norm (even before the Court has the chance to review the merits of the injunction); 

(v)              However, the Defendant may “oppose” to the automatic prohibition of implementation of the norm by serving a “grounded resolution” to the Court, whereby it recognizes that the delay in implementing the norm in the particular case would be seriously detrimental to the public interest; The automatic prohibition to implement the norm is lifted upon the notification of the “grounded resolution” to the Court and the applicant (+we do not know if this has occurred in this particular case, but, again, would be relevant to know+);

(vi)             Despite the prohibition to implement the norm (limited to the particular case), when deciding on the merits of the injunction, the Court may (or may not) adopt the interim measure and suspend (or not) the norm. If the court rejects the interim measure (i.e., decides to not suspend the norm), then the automatic suspension is lifted; The appeal against this Court decision does not have suspensive effects (meaning that the suspensive effective will not remain in place in case of appeal against the decision that rejects the interim measures);

(vii)           The injunction is an accessory measure that requires a main proceeding to be filed; If the main proceeding is not filed, the injunction – and its legal consequences – will expiry (+it would also be important to know if the main proceeding has been brought by the operator in the case here at stake+)

(viii)          If the Court considerers that the use of the injunction has been abusive, it may impose aggravated court fees. 

We have made a preliminary review of the Regional Legislative Decree nr. 14/2020/M and our understanding is that article 11 applies specifically to fleet owners (and not to “operators of electronic platforms”)

Nonetheless, “operators of electronic platforms” are generally required to ensure the full and permanent compliance of the requirements applicable to the activity regulated by Regional Legislative Decree nr. 14/2020/M, including requirements that relate to the provision of TVDE services. If “operators of electronic platforms” fail to do so, the regulator may impose sanctions to the “operators of electronic platforms”, such as suspension, limitation or cessation of the activity. 

The above sets the general framework that we will need to consider to answer to your specific questions. These are preliminary remarks, as the matter and the situation requires profound review of numerous pieces of legislation and there are some court decisions (notable Constitutional Court decisions) related to Regional Legislative Decree nr. 14/2020/M that we will need to consider.

To review the subject and provide an opinion we propose a fixed fee of EUR 3,000.

We expect to deliver our analysis within 2-3 days after acceptance of the quote.

